  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Cause
    
    The Collapse was not a single event but the terminal phase of a structural failure decades in the making. The Star∴eternal spires — aurora-channeling infrastructure central to the Promenade Ward's commercial district — had been routing aurora-light through the Ward's crystal framework at volumes beyond design tolerance for years. When aurora-dimming reduced the light supply, the channeling paths did not simply go dormant. They fractured the ice-shelf beneath the merchant quarter in branching, recursive patterns that precisely mirrored those paths. The ice did not break randomly. It broke along the lines the colony had carved into it.
    
    Light-Regent Lianoshing, whose dual expertise in aurora-crystal and fire-core sciences positioned him to calculate the failure's timeline, warned Aurora Stack's elders on three separate occasions. Each warning was dismissed. The chronicle sources do not record the elders' reasoning; one suspects it was financial.
    
    ## Event
    
    The fracture occurred during pre-dawn hours of a dimming cycle. The Veil☽ Glow arrays that regulated temperature across the Ward shattered in cascading sequence, rendering whole sections uninhabitable. Fire-cores failed in order of provenance: deep-mined cores from the Nightshelf's most unstable veins — the Rofundis∴rupt, Profun∴lapsum, and Abys∴uina shafts, names already synonymous with extraction disaster — exhausted first, followed by stockpiled reserves, then individual merchant braziers.
    
    Seventeen penguins died. The figure appears in only the later accounts and I have not been able to verify it against ice-memory, though it is specific enough to suggest an actual count rather than estimation. Among the dead: the Chowei clutch — five chicks and their dam, traders in aurora-silk — whose brazier failed last.
    
    ## Aftermath
    
    The economic collapse was total. Trade networks built on crystal infrastructure ceased when that infrastructure became the problem. Kaela Stormfeather, who operated the aurora-silk exchange for seventeen years, was found three days after the fracture still arranging fabric samples in her destroyed stall. She withdrew from public life entirely.
    
    Nightshelf miners arrived to assist. The chronicle accounts note they would not meet Aurora eyes — a detail that recurs in every source and likely reflects awareness on both sides that the fire-core supply chain's instability had contributed to the failure. The Collapse is commemorated in the Dawn Mandate, the Long-dark Edict, and the Tide Custom — three separate legal instruments, which suggests the political consequences fractured along lines as recursive as the ice.
